Background. A topological group is called precompact (or totally bounded) if it is (topologically isomorphic to) a subgroup of some compact group; equivalently, its completion is compact. A topological space is separable if it has a countable dense subset, and a Baire space if the intersection of any countable family of dense open sets is dense. A Hausdorff abelian topological group G is reflexive (Pontryagin reflexive) if the canonical homomorphism from G into its bidual G^^ is a topological isomorphism, where the dual G^ = Hom(G, T) is endowed with the compact-open topology. Let T = R/Z denote the circle group and c the cardinality of the continuum.

It is known that there exists an uncountable dense subgroup G of the compact group T^c with covering dimension dim G = 0 such that every countable subgroup of G is closed in G and every uncountable subgroup of G is dense in G; consequently every Hausdorff quotient group of G is either trivial or non-separable.

Problem. Does there exist a precompact abelian topological group G with the property that every Hausdorff quotient group of G is either trivial or non-separable, and which in addition satisfies at least one of the following:
(a) G is connected;
(b) G is a Baire space;
(c) G is reflexive (i.e., the canonical evaluation map G → G^^ is a topological isomorphism, where G^ carries the compact-open topology)?

